<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second International</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Socialist International</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second International, formally the Socialist International, was a federation of socialist and labour parties founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1889 and dominated by the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It established International Workers' Day on 1 May, popularised the demand for an eight-hour day, expelled the anarchists and condemned revisionism at its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> Congress of 1904. From 1905 the prevention of war became its central task, and its congresses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Basel called for working-class action against war. In 1914 its major parties rallied to their nations' war efforts and it collapsed, succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> and the Labour and Socialist International.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-international.jpg" alt="Second International" width="330" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congresso_Socialdem_1910.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By the 1880s rapid industrialisation, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, had created a new urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, and the extension of the vote made mass parliamentary parties of the working class possible. The strongest was the Social Democratic Party of Germany, founded in 1875 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> by a merger of Marxist and Lassallean groups and led by Wilhelm Liebknecht and August Bebel. French socialism was more divided, among several factions.</p><p>The demand for an eight-hour day was taken up by French socialists, and the centenary of the French Revolution in 1889 offered an occasion for a congress. Because of the French divisions two rival congresses met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>: the Possibilists, with the British Trades Union Congress, in the Rue de Lancry, and the Marxists, led by Jules Guesde and supported by the SPD, in the Salle Petrelle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early congresses were absorbed by the struggle against the anarchists. After their exclusion the International had to face the question of revolution or reform. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a> argued from 1896 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s predictions of collapse had proved wrong and that the movement should embrace gradual reform. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the socialist Alexandre Millerand joined the government of Pierre Waldeck-Rousseau in June 1899, causing a great controversy. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1904 Jean Jaurès and August Bebel debated, and the congress condemned revisionism and ministerialism.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a>, preventing war became its central task, and the congresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> (1907), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> (1910) and Basel (1912) passed ever more urgent resolutions. The containment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> brought optimism, and by 1913 many leaders believed the threat of war had receded. The International was caught off guard by the crisis of July 1914. Jaurès was assassinated, and the major parties supported their governments in the war, a split between pro-war majorities and an anti-war minority that prefigured the later division between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Second International was a loose federation of autonomous national parties and trade unions, with no executive machinery in its first decade; the London Congress of 1896 affirmed the autonomy of national groups. The Paris Congress of 1900 created the International Socialist Bureau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, which grew in authority under its secretary Camille Huysmans after 1905 but remained an organ of correlation, not direction.</p><p>The conference at Bern in February 1919 failed to revive it, and it was succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> in 1919 and the Labour and Socialist International in 1923. Lenin called its collapse a betrayal by opportunist leaders; Georges Haupt argued that it came from the International's long-standing contradictions, among them parties with no means of enforcing joint action.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second International” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Second International</b>, founded in Paris in July 1889, called for demonstrations for the eight-hour day on 1 May 1890, a date proposed by the American Federation of Labor: American unions had struck for it on 1 May 1886, days before the Haymarket affair in Chicago.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After bitter disputes the Marxists expelled the Bakuninists at the Hague Congress of 1872, and the anarchists formed the St. Imier International; they were expelled from the <b>Second International</b> too, in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The song's success went with that of the <b>Second International</b>, which adopted it as its official anthem, and by the 1910 International Socialist Congress in Copenhagen it had been sung in 18 languages.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li></ul></section>
